Page 63 of the 'Analysis situs (deuxième mouture)' notes. The top numeral is very likely '63', not '13'. Grothendieck is building an axiomatic, combinatorial framework (an 'Atlas') for tame or stratified geometric 'figures'. The data are:
- a set M of 'multiplicités' (elementary pieces, roughly cells or strata);
- two order-type relations ≤ (sub-figure/inclusion) and ≪ (a stronger relation, roughly 'contained in the closure/interior');
- a symmetric incidence-type relation written ≭, which is evidently the relation axiomatised in At 9, At 9' and At 9'';
- a set L of 'lieux' (minimal or point-like elements);
- for each X its shadow omb(X) ⊂ L.

The page opens mid-sentence. In 'set-theoretic' (ensembliste) situations, where everything is determined by shadows, one would like to describe the main ingredients of the Atlas, via (M, ≤, ≪, ≭), purely in set-theoretic terms using only the restricted relation ≭_L on L. This is natural because, for two lieux x, y, (1.59) holds:
  x ∥ y ⇔ x ≭ y and x ≠ y.
So on L the relation ≭_L is just ∥_L (strict 'parallelism' or separation) plus the diagonal, and the two carry the same information.

This would put special weight on ≭_L (equivalently ∥_L). Grothendieck judges it reasonable in set-theoretic cases. 'Le seul hic' is that it fails in the most important interesting examples: non-ensembliste Atlases where a multiplicity is not determined by its shadow of lieux. He therefore falls back on a weaker property 'qui fera le même usage', labelled in the margin 'Variante "simplifiée" de At 9''.

At 9'': let X, Y ∈ M and F = X ∩ Y as in At 9'. Then X ≭ Y holds if and only if both:
(i) for all x ∈ omb X − omb F and all y ∈ omb Y − omb F, x ∥ y;
(ii) F is empty or 'élémentaire', i.e. F ∈ M, a common multiplicity.

Relation to the previous page: At 9' was the one-directional criterion (a pointwise condition on the shadows outside omb F implies X ⋔ Y), with a converse promised via a later 'disposition' axiom (p. 72). At 9'' turns this into an equivalence by adding the requirement that the intersection be a single cell or empty. This is the familiar 'intersection of two closed cells is a face or empty' condition from regular/polyhedral complexes, combined with a pointwise criterion on vertices.

The boxed NB says a variant with other conditions can be given, apparently without (or for) the order on the figure F̃ (reading uncertain). The next page states that At 9 is implied by At 9' plus At 9''. It groups At 8–At 10 as 'axiomes de liaison' and moves on to Inf^≪ of sub-figures.
